/arXiv.2601.17349\n\nAuthors:\n\nHailong Yan\n\n  * University of Electronic Science and Technology of China\n\nShice Liu\n\nShice Liu\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nXiangtao Zhang\n\nXiangtao Zhang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nLujian Yao\n\nLujian Yao\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn the current era of mobile internet, Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement (L3IE) is critical for mobile devices, which faces a persistent trade-off between visual quality and model compactness. While recent methods employ disentangling strategies to simplify lightweight architectural design, such as Retinex theory and YUV color space transformations, their performance is fundamentally limited by overlooking channel-specific degradation patterns and cross-channel interactions. To address this gap, we perform a frequency-domain analysis that confirms the superiority of the YUV color space for L3IE. We identify a key insight: the Y channel primarily loses low-frequency content, while the UV channels are corrupted by high-frequency noise. Leveraging this finding, we propose a novel YUV-based paradigm that strategically restores channels using a Dual-Stream Global-Local Attention module for the Y channel, a Y-guided Local-Aware Frequency Attention module for the UV channels, and a Guided Interaction module for final feature fusion. Extensive experiments validate that our model establishes a new state-of-the-art on multiple benchmarks, delivering superior visual quality with a significantly lower parameter count.\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (0)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any references for this publication.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective (https://www.catalyzex.com/paper/revisiting-lightweight-low-light-image)\nciteturn38search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Title:Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective\n\n# Title:Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective\n\n### Paper and Code\n\nRequest Code\n\n\nJan 24, 2026\n\nAuthors:Hailong Yan, Shice Liu, Xiangtao Zhang, Lujian Yao, Fengxiang Yang, Jinwei Chen, Bo Li\n\nFigures and Tables:\n\n[Input: Ask the author(s) a question! :)]\n\nAsk\n\nAbstract:In the current era of mobile internet, Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement (L3IE) is critical for mobile devices, which faces a persistent trade-off between visual quality and model compactness. While recent methods employ disentangling strategies to simplify lightweight architectural design, such as Retinex theory and YUV color space transformations, their performance is fundamentally limited by overlooking channel-specific degradation patterns and cross-channel interactions. To address this gap, we perform a frequency-domain analysis that confirms the superiority of the YUV color space for L3IE. We identify a key insight: the Y channel primarily loses low-frequency content, while the UV channels are corrupted by high-frequency noise. Leveraging this finding, we propose a novel YUV-based paradigm that strategically restores channels using a Dual-Stream Global-Local Attention module for the Y channel, a Y-guided Local-Aware Frequency Attention module for the UV channels, and a Guided Interaction module for final feature fusion. Extensive experiments validate that our model establishes a new state-of-the-art on multiple benchmarks, delivering superior visual quality with a significantly lower parameter count.\n\n* Tech report  \n\nFull-text Links:View paper on--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Hailong Yan (https://dblp.org/pid/193/1721.html)\nciteturn38search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective.\n\n## Informal and Other Publications\n\n  * 2026\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i7]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2601-17349\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2601-17349\n\nHailong Yan, Shice Liu, Xiangtao Zhang, Lujian Yao, Fengxiang Yang, Jinwei Chen, Bo Li:\nRevisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective. CoRR abs/2601.17349 (2026)\n\n[i6]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/revisiting-lightweight-low-light-image-enhancement)\nciteturn38search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective ...       title = {{Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective}},\n\n# Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective\n\nHailong Yan, Shice Liu, Xiangtao Zhang, Lujian Yao, Fengxiang Yang, Jinwei Chen, Bo LiarXiv 2026\n\n## TL;DR\n\nLightweight low-light image enhancement on mobile devices typically forces a harsh trade-off between image quality and model size. By analyzing low-light degradation in the frequency domain, this paper demonstrates that the YUV color space naturally separates brightness loss from color noise. Leveraging this insight, the authors design a tailored, asymmetric network that achieves state-of-the-art enhancement using just 30K parameters—making it exceptionally well-suited for real-time edge deployment.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nEnhancing low-light images in real time on resource-constrained devices (like smartphones, drones, and dashcams) is a notoriously difficult task. Traditional deep learning approaches process images in the standard RGB color space. However, in low-light conditions, both noise and brightness degradation are heavily entangled across all three RGB channels, making it computationally expensive for a lightweight network to fix both simultaneously.\n\nWhile some methods attempt to bypass this by using Retinex theory (splitting an image into illumination and reflectance), this decomposition is mathematically ill-posed and often requires large, complex networks to avoid unrealistic lighting artifacts. Other methods turn to the YUV color space to separate luminance (Y) from chrominance (UV), but they typically process all channels uniformly or completely in isolation, leading to color shifts, bleeding, or residual noise.\n\n## Methods\n\nThe authors propose a novel, asymmetric framework that converts low-light RGB images into the YUV color space and applies targeted operations to each channel based on how they actually degrade.\n\nImage: Overview of the proposed YUV-based enhancement frameworkExpand\n\nFigure 2: The overview of the proposed method. The Y channel is enhanced via DSGLA, while the UV channels are refined by LAFA with guidance from Y features. The GI module fuses luminance and chrominance features for consistent enhancement.\n\nThe architecture consists of three core modules:\n\n  1. Dual-Stream Global-Local Attention (DSGLA): Dedicated to the luminance (Y) channel. It uses a channel-splitting design to handle different structural components. A global branch utilizes Dilated-Depth Self Attention (DDSA) to efficiently capture long-range, low-frequency brightness structures. Meanwhile, a local branch uses Ghost-Gated Aggregation (GGA) to recover fine-grained, high-frequency spatial details.\n  2. Local-Aware Frequency Attention (LAFA): Dedicated to the chrominance (UV) channels. Instead of processing color blindly, LAFA uses the enhanced Y-channel features as a structural guide. It applies a Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) to modulate both the amplitude and phase spectrums, effectively filtering out high-frequency color noise while preserving structural alignment.\n  3. Guided Interaction (GI): Fuses the enhanced Y and UV features back together. It employs a Cross-Guide Mixer (CGM) for bidirectional feature alignment and a Fusion Alignment (FA) module to eliminate cross-channel redundancy, ensuring that the final output maintains strict color-luminance consistency.\n\n## Datasets\n\nThe model was trained and evaluated across a mix of standard paired and unpaired datasets:\n\n  * Paired Datasets: LOLv1, LOLv2 (divided into Real and Synthetic subsets), and LSRW (which contains real-world low-light images captured via Nikon and Huawei hardware).\n  * Unpaired Datasets: DICM, LIME, MEF, NPE, and VV (used to test zero-shot generalization performance).\n\n## Key Insights and Ideas\n\n  * The YUV Disentanglement Advantage: Through frequency-domain analysis, the authors show that low-light degradation manifests very differently across YUV channels. The Y channel primarily suffers from a loss of low-frequency energy (global brightness), whereas the UV channels are predominantly corrupted by high-frequency noise (color distortion).\nImage: Comparison of degradation patterns across RGB and YUV spacesExpand\n\nFigure 1: Comparison of degradation patterns across RGB and YUV color spaces under low-light conditions. The first two rows show the low-light input images and their corresponding frequency spectrums (via Fourier Transform), while the last two rows show the ground truth (GT) images and their spectrums. Unlike the RGB channel-entangled degradation, YUV channels exhibit clearer disentangled: the Y channel mainly shows low-frequency luminance degradation, accompanied by potential high-frequency noise, while the U/V channels contain more prominent high-frequency chrominance noise.\n\n  * Phase Matters for Structure: While many frequency-domain networks only manipulate the amplitude spectrum to denoise images, this paper proves that the phase spectrum contains critical structural alignment cues. Swapping the phase spectrum of a low-light image with a ground-truth image retains the underlying structural details, justifying why the LAFA module modulates both components.\nImage: Visualization of spectrum swapping experiments showing the importance of phaseExpand\n\nFigure 3: Visualization of spectrum swapping experiments.\n\n  * Asymmetric Processing Wins: Because the degradation patterns are decoupled in YUV, uniform processing is highly inefficient. Tailoring specific network blocks to handle low-frequency restoration in Y and high-frequency denoising in UV allows the model to shed massive amounts of parameter weight.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Unprecedented Compactness: The model achieves state-of-the-art performance with only 30K parameters (0.03M) and 1.45 GFLOPs. This makes it significantly smaller than existing lightweight models like LYTNet (45K parameters) and RetinexMac (2.29M parameters).\n  * Quantitative Performance: On the benchmark LOLv2-Real dataset, the model achieved a top-tier PSNR of 31.181 dB, outperforming several heavy, large-scale networks.\n  * Real-Time Latency: The model clocks an impressive latency of just 6.5 ms on a GPU and 124.1 ms on a CPU, making it fully viable for real-time mobile deployment.\n  * Visual Quality: Qualitatively, the approach suppresses grain and color noise in dark regions exceptionally well without causing the overexposure or unnatural color shifts seen in competing models.\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\nThe authors acknowledge that while their work thoroughly validates the benefits of the YUV color space over standard RGB, its optimality relative to other alternative color spaces (such as HSV, LAB, or recently proposed spaces like HVI) remains an open question. Investigating how these alternative color representations handle frequency-domain degradation is highlighted as a promising avenue for future lightweight enhancement research.\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @misc{yan2026revisiting,\n      author = {Hailong Yan and Shice Liu and Xiangtao Zhang and Lujian Yao and Fengxiang Yang and Jinwei Chen and Bo Li},\n      title = {{Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective}},\n      year = {2026},\n      eprint = {2601.17349},\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n      url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17349},\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJinwei Chen (https://www.csauthors.net/jinwei-chen-003/)\nciteturn38search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Revisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspective.\n\nBidirectional Beta-Tuned Diffusion Model.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nTianyi Zheng\n\n,\n\nJiayang Zou\n\n,\n\nPeng-Tao Jiang\n\n,\n\nHao Zhang\n\n,\n\nJinwei Chen\n\n,\n\nJia Wang\n\n,\n\nBo Li\n\nIEEE Trans. Pattern Anal. Mach. Intell., January, 2026\n\nRevisiting Lightweight Low-Light Image Enhancement: From a YUV Color Space Perspect